    subjectId: 'economics', subjectLabel: 'Economics', level: 'ordinary',
    questionRef: '2025 OL Q13(a)(i)',
    stem: 'Ireland remains close to full employment with some employers experiencing shortages of skilled labour in different sectors of the economy. Explain the factor of production labour.',
    commandWord: 'Explain',
    demand: 'Give the textbook definition of labour as a factor of production: human effort applied to the production of goods and services. The scheme rewards one complete economic statement that names BOTH halves — (1) the human effort that people contribute and (2) that this effort goes into producing goods/services (wealth). Write a full defining sentence, not just an example or a job title.',
    trap: 'This is one 7-mark explanation graded as a single statement — there is no part-credit ladder shown, so a vague half-answer is risky. The scheme answer is \'The effort that people contribute (human effort) to the production of goods and services (wealth).\' Two killers: (1) describing the wrong factor — labour is human effort, NOT the risk-taking entrepreneur (the scheme defines that factor separately as the one who \'takes a risk… in return for profit\'), so a \'risk/reward\' answer scores 0 here; (2) only naming a worker or job (e.g. \'people who work in a factory\') instead of stating the economic function — an example without the definition does not earn the explain mark.',
