    subjectId: 'construction-studies', subjectLabel: 'Construction Studies', level: 'higher',
    questionRef: '2024 HL · Q5(a)',
    stem: 'The sketch shows a proposed external wall for a new house, constructed using Insulated Concrete Formwork (ICF). Calculate the U-value of the wall, given the construction sequence and data: acrylic external render 12 mm (k = 0.670), external insulation 150 mm (k = 0.030), concrete 150 mm (k = 1.280), internal insulation 50 mm (k = 0.030), internal plasterboard 12.5 mm (k = 0.250) and internal skim coat 4 mm (r = 5.550); external surface resistance 0.048 and internal surface resistance 0.122 m²°C/W.',
    commandWord: 'Calculate',
    demand: 'Tabulate the thermal resistance of every layer (R = T/k, or R = T×r for the skim coat) plus both surface resistances, sum to ΣR = 7.044, then apply U = 1/ΣR = 1/7.044 = 0.142 and write the answer with units W/m²K. The two insulation layers (0.150/0.030 = 5.000 and 0.050/0.030 = 1.667) dominate ΣR — get their division right or the whole answer drifts.',
    trap: 'The scheme awards the U-value as a per-line tabulation (11 × 3 marks): the six material-layer resistances (render, external insulation, concrete, internal insulation, plasterboard, skim coat), the two surface resistances, the total resistance and the final U-value each carry their own 3-mark line. Quoting a bare U-value with no tabulated working, or omitting the W/m²K (W/m²°C) units on the final answer, forfeits award lines — and converting the skim-coat resistivity r = 5.550 as if it were a conductivity (dividing instead of T×r) corrupts ΣR and the answer.',
    source: 'LC Construction Studies 2024 HL marking scheme, Q5(a) ("(a) U-value of external wall (11 × 3 marks)"; "U = 1 / 7.044 = 0.142 W/m² °C")',
    marks: 33,
