    id: 'cons-calculate-roof-uvalue-hl',
    subjectId: 'construction-studies', subjectLabel: 'Construction Studies', level: 'higher',
    questionRef: '2023 HL · Q5(a)',
    stem: 'An extension to a house has a highly insulated flat roof with a waterproof roof covering. Calculate the U-value of the roof, given the construction sequence and data: waterproof covering 2 mm (r = 6.250), roof insulation 150 mm (k = 0.022), plywood decking 20 mm (k = 0.130), a clear cavity between the roof joists 200 mm (R = 0.170), ceiling insulation 50 mm (k = 0.022) and plasterboard 12.5 mm (k = 0.250); external surface resistance 0.048 and internal surface resistance 0.122 m²°C/W.',
    commandWord: 'Calculate',
    demand: 'Resolve every element to a resistance: waterproof covering via R = T×r (0.002 × 6.250 = 0.0125), and the conductivity layers via R = T/k — roof insulation 0.150/0.022 = 6.818, plywood 0.020/0.130 = 0.154, ceiling insulation 0.050/0.022 = 2.273, plasterboard 0.0125/0.250 = 0.050 — then add the given resistances for the clear cavity (0.170) and both surfaces (0.048 and 0.122). Summing all eight gives ΣR = 9.647, so U = 1/9.647 = 0.104 W/m²°C. The cavity and the waterproof covering are supplied as a resistance R and a resistivity r respectively, not as conductivities.',
    trap: 'The scheme tabulates this as (10 × 3 marks): each layer resistance, the total resistance and the final U-value are separate 3-mark lines. Dividing the clear-cavity 0.170 (already a resistance R) by its thickness, or treating the waterproof covering\'s resistivity r = 6.250 as a k-value, breaks ΣR; and a final answer with no W/m²°C units forfeits the dedicated \'calculation of U-value\' line even when ΣR is correct.',
    source: 'LC Construction Studies 2023 HL marking scheme, Q5(a) ("(a) U-value of flat roof (10 × 3 marks)"; "U = 1 / 9.647 = 0.104 W/m2 0C")',
    marks: 30,
