    subjectId: 'applied-mathematics', subjectLabel: 'Applied Mathematics', level: 'higher',
    questionRef: '2023 HL · Q4(vi)',
    stem: 'A ball of mass m kg is projected vertically downwards at 15 m s⁻¹ into a tank of water, where it experiences an upward buoyancy force 4 times its weight and a drag force of mv² N opposing its motion. While moving downwards it satisfies v dv/ds = −29.4 − v². After reaching its maximum depth the ball begins to move upwards through the water. Write down a differential equation for the rate of change of the velocity v of the ball while it moves upwards through the water.',
    commandWord: 'Write down',
    demand: 'The equation itself, immediately and exactly — no derivation is demanded, but the unseen force analysis must be redone for the new direction. Taking up as positive: buoyancy 4mg up, weight mg down, drag mv² now DOWN (it opposes the upward motion), so ma = 4mg − mg − mv², giving dv/dt (or v dv/ds) = 3g − v² = 29.4 − v². That single correct equation is the whole 5-mark award.',
    trap: 'Recycling the downward-motion equation v dv/ds = −29.4 − v² with the signs untouched, or flipping the wrong term to get 29.4 + v², wrecks the item: on the way up the buoyancy–weight resultant (+3g = +29.4) now drives the ball while drag still opposes the motion (−v²). The entire award sits on that one printed line, and under the scheme\'s penalties a sign error is a full blunder (−3 of the 5) — \'Write down\' means there is no multi-step method trail to absorb it, so only the exact equation 29.4 − v² earns the full 5.',
    source: 'LC Applied Mathematics 2023 HL marking scheme, Q4(vi) ("dv/dt [= v dv/ds] = 3g − v² = 29.4 − v²" — 5 marks; preamble: blunder −3)',
    marks: 5,
